      "math_academy_id": "q-43048",
      "sequence_position": 22,
      "problem": "Consider the matrices given below. Given that $A$ and $B$ are row equivalent matrices and $det(A)=6$, find $det(B).$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 6 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]?\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that $B$ can be obtained from $A$ by swapping the $2$nd and the $3$rd rows. \n\nThis changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =-det(A) \\\\  & =-(6) \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
